  "nameStory": "The indigo milk cap gets its striking name from its deep blue color that looks like natural indigo dye. When you cut or break the mushroom, it oozes a brilliant blue 'milk' or latex, making it one of the most distinctive mushrooms you'll ever encounter in New England's forests.",
  "description": "The indigo milk cap is a stunning blue mushroom that's impossible to mistake for anything else when fresh. It has a classic mushroom shape with a cap that ranges from 4-15 cm (2-6 inches) across and sits on a sturdy stem 2-8 cm tall. Fresh specimens are a rich, deep blue color, though older mushrooms fade to pale blue-gray. What makes this fungus truly special is the bright blue 'milk' that oozes out when you cut or damage the tissue.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Milk/Latex",
      "description": "When cut or broken, the mushroom oozes bright indigo-blue milk that slowly turns green when exposed to air - this is the most reliable identifying feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Deep blue when fresh, fading to pale blue-gray with age; surface feels slightly sticky when moist and may have concentric zones or rings",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "Blue gills that run slightly down the stem, spaced moderately apart, and may show darker blue spots where damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Solid blue stem, sometimes with darker blue pits or spots, feels firm and substantial when fresh",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Cream to pale yellow spore print - collect by placing the cap gills-down on white paper overnight",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, pleasant earthy smell with no strong or unpleasant odors",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for indigo milk caps in both deciduous and coniferous forests throughout New England, where they form beneficial partnerships (mycorrhizal relationships) with tree roots. They typically fruit from the soil in mixed woodlands, especially areas with oak, pine, or hemlock trees. You'll often find them scattered individually or in small groups on forest floors with good leaf litter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Generally not fruiting yet, though mycelium is active underground forming connections with tree roots.",
    "summer": "Prime fruiting season begins, especially after warm summer rains when soil moisture is adequate.",
    "fall": "Peak season for finding fresh specimens, particularly from late summer through early fall when conditions are moist and temperatures moderate.",
    "winter": "Fruiting season ends, though old weathered specimens might persist briefly into early winter in protected areas."
  },
  "funFact": "The indigo milk cap is considered a gourmet edible mushroom and is actually sold in rural markets across Mexico, Guatemala, and China - the blue color remains even after cooking, creating dishes that look almost otherworldly!",
  "confusionNotes": "When fresh and blue, this mushroom is virtually impossible to confuse with anything else in New England. However, very old, faded specimens that have lost their blue color could potentially be mistaken for other pale milkcaps, but a scratch test will still reveal traces of the characteristic blue-green latex.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "While this mushroom is edible and prized in many cultures, always remember the golden rule of mushroom foraging: never eat any wild mushroom without 100% positive identification confirmed by a local expert, even one as distinctive as this.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's many milkcap species, the indigo milk cap is unique for its blue coloration and blue latex. Other milkcaps in our region produce white, yellow, orange, or red latex - like the saffron milkcap with its orange milk or Peck's milky cap with its white milk that turns yellow. The indigo's blue milk that turns green is a dead giveaway that sets it apart from all its Lactarius relatives.",
